    "The roots of the genre, though, stay Dominican. Our <a href=\"/en/bouyon-soca.html\">bouyon soca</a> page recalls the lineage kept by this site: bouyon soca was started by Dada in Dominica in 2003, per the first hand testimony of Sean “Smokiller” Francis, who lived that scene from the inside. Two decades later, the next wave runs through hundreds of releases like “FETE”, whose writers do not always claim an island and whose listings sometimes fit in four lines. That is also why this site spells the genre with care: Wrecketeng, for example, is the official spelling of the historic Dominican current, while “reketeng” is only a widespread internet misspelling.",
    "No opened source ties “FETE” to an event, a specific carnival, a named label or a live stage. The record carries no island hashtag, no place mention and no label credit in its listing. Its verifiable circuit is therefore exactly the one its two pages describe: the YouTube release, the DistroKid page of September 2026, and the @AcceCarter handle as the rallying point. Any more precise attachment, to an island, a city or a diaspora, will wait for a source that states it.",
    "That restraint has a useful counterpart for the reader discovering the name here: it makes what comes next easy to verify. The day Acce Carter publishes a second record, it will be enough to reopen his pages and compare the credits: same producer or new team, same single handle or finally named accounts, origin stated or still unsaid. The day an artist page appears on a platform, with a photo and a biography, the file will change in nature and this page will change with it, keeping the dated trace of the thin state of October 2026. That is the rule of every page in this sweep: photograph the scene as it is published, no bigger, no smaller, and let the artists write what follows through their releases."
